Sembcorp Bug Bounty Program — Program Policy
Last updated: 15 May, 2026
Overview
Welcome to the Sembcorp Bug Bounty Program. We appreciate your interest in helping us improve the security of our products. Please read through the guidelines below before beginning any research activity. Participation in this program means you have agreed to these terms.
This program covers two tracks:
Web Application — https://sandbox.sembcorp.com/
Mobile Application — Integra Global (available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store)
Part A — Web Application
Before You Start
All requests during the bug bounty period must include the following HTTP header:
x-hackerone-research: [your H1 username]
Example: x-hackerone-research: Jeremy123T
Note: Requests without the x-hackerone-research headers may be blocked by our perimeter security controls. A rate limit of 200 requests (per minute) applies — requests beyond this threshold will be throttled.
1. Program Overview
This program invites security researchers to responsibly test specific, publicly accessible pages of the Sembcorp corporate website. It follows a black-box testing model — no source code, credentials, or internal knowledge will be provided. Only publicly accessible content is in scope.
2. Safe Harbor
Sembcorp authorises security research conducted within the boundaries of this policy. Researchers acting in good faith, staying within scope, and avoiding disruption or data exposure will not face legal action.
2.1 Our Commitments to Researchers
Commitment	Details
Acknowledgement	Valid submissions will be acknowledged via HackerOne
Review	Reports will be assessed in good faith
Communication	Status updates will be provided through HackerOne
Remediation Notice	Researchers will be notified when relevant fixes are implemented
All remediation decisions and timelines remain at Sembcorp's discretion.
3. Rules of Engagement
3.1 Data Protection
Do not access, retain, copy, or destroy:
Customer or employee personal data
Unpublished or pre-release website content
Confidential or proprietary Sembcorp information
If you accidentally encounter any such data, stop testing immediately and submit a report via HackerOne without exploring further.
3.2 Access Control
If you inadvertently gain access to any of the following, report it immediately and do not explore further:
Non-public webpages, applications, or administrative interfaces
Non-public credentials, secrets, tokens, or API keys
3.3 Service Integrity
All testing must be non-destructive and low-impact. Please do not:
Degrade the website's user experience or disrupt services of in-scope assets.
Modify, corrupt, or delete any data of in-scope assets.
Perform denial of service (DoS/DDoS) testing
Alter live content when demonstrating a proof-of-concept
3.4 Scope Compliance
Test only assets listed under Section 4: In-Scope Assets
If scope is unclear, do not proceed — submit via HackerOne with your scope reasoning included
Thoughtful submissions that are ultimately out of scope may be marked Not Applicable rather than rejected outright
3.5 Reporting Requirements
All submissions must be made via HackerOne and include:
Step-by-step reproduction instructions
Screenshots, screen recordings, or relevant request/response data
A proof-of-concept demonstrating exploitability
Recommended mitigations (encouraged, not mandatory)
Please do not use third-party file-sharing services.
3.6 Account Usage
Test only accounts you personally own. Do not attempt to access other users' accounts or data.
3.7 Data Retention
Securely delete any Sembcorp data that was downloaded or cached during testing once your report has been submitted.
3.8 Non-Qualifying Findings — Static Pages
The following are not eligible for rewards on static, non-interactive pages, grouped by category for ease of reference:
General / Low-Impact
Spam or phishing reports
Security observations without a demonstrable exploit
Low-risk (P3) issues
Self-XSS
Content spoofing without actionable impact
IDN homograph issues
Tabnabbing
Client-Side Behaviour
Clickjacking on non-interactive pages
CSRF without authentication or impact
HTML/link injection without exploitable impact
Browser autocomplete or password-saving behaviour
Missing Secure or HttpOnly flags on non-sensitive cookies
CSV/Excel formula injection
Information Disclosure
Descriptive error messages that don't expose sensitive data
HTTP 404 or non-200 responses
Fingerprinting or banner disclosure of public-facing infrastructure
Disclosure of publicly available files or directories (e.g., robots.txt)
Mixed content warnings without sensitive data exposure
Authentication & Accounts (not applicable — no accounts exist)
Forced login/logout CSRF
Account lockout or brute force
Username/email enumeration
CAPTCHA-related issues
Brute-forcing of intended non-interactive functionality
Transport & Infrastructure
Missing HTTP security headers (HSTS, X-Frame-Options, CSP, X-XSS-Protection, X-Content-Type-Options)
OPTIONS HTTP method enabled
SSL/TLS issues without a realistic exploit path
Weak cipher suites without demonstrable exploitability
Email configuration issues (SPF, DKIM, DMARC)
Networking protocol issues outside Sembcorp's control
Third-Party & Out-of-Scope
Issues originating from third-party platforms not owned by Sembcorp
Issues affecting outdated or unsupported browsers
Subdomain takeovers for domains not owned by Sembcorp
Vulnerable client-side libraries without an exploit path
Automated scan output without manual validation
3.9 Non-Qualifying Findings — Interactive / Dynamic Pages
The following are not eligible for rewards unless a clear proof of exploitability and measurable impact is demonstrated, grouped by category for ease of reference:
General / Observations Only
Security observations without exploitability
Missing security headers without a demonstrated exploit
Generic information disclosure without sensitive data exposure
UI/UX or cosmetic issues unrelated to security
Duplicate reports or multiple reports from the same root cause
Automated scanner output without manual validation
Client-Side & Interaction Issues
Clickjacking on pages without sensitive or privileged actions
CSRF on endpoints without sensitive state-changing actions
Self-XSS or issues requiring unreasonable user interaction
Open redirects without demonstrated credential theft or sensitive impact
Session or logout/login CSRF without significant security consequence
Authentication & Account Controls
Rate-limiting claims without evidence of exploitability
Account enumeration with minimal or speculative impact
Password policy suggestions without exploit evidence
CAPTCHA weaknesses that don't enable meaningful abuse
Transport & Infrastructure
Weak TLS/cipher findings without practical exploitability
Third-Party & Out-of-Scope
Vulnerable library reports without a proof of exploit
Issues in third-party scripts or integrations not owned by Sembcorp
