7. Prohibited Testing Activities
The following are not permitted under this program:
Testing of third-party or vendor systems
Social engineering or phishing
Physical security testing
Automated scanning without manual validation
Denial of service (DoS/DDoS) testing
Brute force or credential stuffing
Spam or mass submissions
8. Disclosure Policy
This program operates under a non-disclosure policy by default. Please do not publicly share:
Vulnerability details or proofs of concept
Screenshots or technical specifics
Your participation in the program
Public disclosure requires explicit written approval from Sembcorp.
9. Rewards
Rewards are determined at Sembcorp's sole discretion. Priority is given to findings that are in scope, reproducible, and result in meaningful security improvements.
Submissions generally not eligible for rewards include:
Issues outside the defined in-scope pages or features
Findings requiring unrealistic conditions or excessive user manipulation
Reports without a credible attack scenario or meaningful security impact
Low-impact informational findings that don't require remediation
Third-party platform issues not operated or controlled by Sembcorp
Duplicate reports sharing the same underlying root cause
9.1 Researcher Eligibility
To be eligible for a reward, researchers must:
Have personally discovered and responsibly disclosed the vulnerability
Be the first to submit a valid report for the issue or its root cause
Have complied with this policy, applicable laws, and HackerOne's rules
Have no conflicts of interest with Sembcorp or its related entities
Be legally eligible to receive a reward under applicable laws and regulations
Part B — Mobile Application (Integra Global Mobile Application for Google Play Store and Sembcorp Safety Buddy Mobile Application for Apple App Store)
Before You Start
The same header requirement applies:
x-hackerone-research: [your H1 username]
A rate limit of 200 requests is also in effect.
⚠️ Logging into the Integra Global Mobile Application for Google Play Store and Sembcorp Safety Buddy Mobile Application for Apple App Store are not permitted. All authentication-related activity is out of scope.
Program Overview
This program covers static and dynamic analysis of the Integra Global Mobile Application and Sembcorp Safety Buddy Mobile Application, downloadable from the Apple App Store and Google Play Store respectively. Researchers may perform:
Reverse engineering
Client-side inspection of application logic, configuration, and local storage
Inspection of embedded resources and runtime behaviour
Rules of Engagement
The same core principles from Part A apply here — data protection, service integrity, scope compliance, reporting requirements, and data deletion (Sections 3.1–3.7).
Additional mobile-specific notes:
No account credentials will be provided
Login and any authenticated activity are out of scope
Do not brute force login to the application
Non-Qualifying Findings — Mobile Application
Device & Platform Controls
Missing or optional jailbreak/root detection
Running the app on emulators or modified devices
Reverse engineering findings without demonstrated security impact
Lack of code obfuscation, symbol stripping, or binary hardening
Readable class, method, or variable names in the app binary
Client-side logic exposure by design
Transport Security
Missing SSL/TLS pinning, unless it demonstrably leads to sensitive data exposure or account compromise
Allowing traffic interception via user-installed CA certificates without proven exploitability
TLS configuration preferences without security impact
Absent certificate transparency enforcement
Authentication (Without Exploitation)
Username/email enumeration without account takeover
Missing CAPTCHA or rate limiting on authentication flows
Password policy observations
Optional MFA without a demonstrated bypass
Brute-force resistance observations without exploitation
Post-Authentication Issues
Authorisation, IDOR, or access-control issues requiring valid credentials
Business logic or workflow flaws requiring login
Privilege escalation assumptions without valid tokens
Session management or logout issues observable only after login
Local Storage (No Sensitive Data)
Storage of non-sensitive data in SharedPreferences, NSUserDefaults, or cache directories
Storage of UI preferences, feature flags, timestamps, or device identifiers
Empty, placeholder, or test data stored locally
Logging & Metadata
Debug logging without disclosure of sensitive data
Logcat/NSLog output containing only benign information
App version or build identifier disclosure
Crash logs or stack traces without sensitive content
Deep Links & URL Schemes
Presence of deep links, app links, or custom URL schemes without exploitability
Deep links that only open public or authentication screens
WebView Configuration
JavaScript-enabled WebViews loading non-sensitive content
Mixed content without an exploit chain
Absent Safe Browsing configuration without security impact
API & Backend (Without Abuse)
Discovery or enumeration of public API endpoints
GraphQL introspection without unauthorised data access
Downloadable OpenAPI/Swagger specifications
APIs correctly returning 401 or 403 responses
HTTP methods protected by proper authentication
Other Low-Impact Findings
Self-XSS or self-inflicted denial of service
Clickjacking without sensitive user actions
Open redirects without meaningful chaining
CSV injection without external exploitation
Rate-limiting observations without demonstrated abuse
DoS scenarios requiring unrealistic traffic volume
Vulnerabilities requiring physical device access
Theoretical observations without demonstrable impact
Client-Side Input Validation
Weaknesses enforced server-side
UI-only restrictions that can't be bypassed server-side
Cosmetic or visual UI inconsistencies
Third-Party SDKs
Use of third-party libraries without a demonstrated exploit
Analytics, crash reporting, or advertising SDKs without impact
Privacy & Permissions
Permission usage aligned with stated app functionality
Privacy observations without unauthorised data access
In-Scope Testing Activities
Testing is limited to the Integra Global Mobile Application for Google Play Store and Sembcorp Safety Buddy Mobile Application for Apple App Store. Permitted activities include:
Static and dynamic analysis of the application package
Reverse engineering and client-side inspection of application logic
Inspection of configuration, local storage, embedded resources, and runtime behaviour
Prohibited Testing Activities
Logging in to the app or performing any authenticated activity
Brute force or credential stuffing
Denial of service (DoS/DDoS) testing
Testing of third-party or vendor systems
Social engineering or phishing
Physical security testing
Automated scanning without manual validation
Spam or mass submissions
